---
// Not a content-collection entry: accounts.toml.example is TOML, not
// markdown, so it never passes through src/content.config.ts. It is read from
// the repo root at build time and rendered as a highlighted reference. A
// missing file must fail the build, not render an empty page — readFileSync
// throws on its own, and that throw is the guard.
//
// This is the same file `init::TEMPLATE` include_str!s into the binary, so the
// page and the tool cannot disagree about what a starting config looks like.
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const source = readFileSync('../docs/accounts.toml.example', 'utf8');
---

<DocsLayout title="Config" headings={[]} current="config">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      This is the one file you write by hand: <code>~/.config/gitwho/accounts.toml</code>, the
      accounts gitwho resolves against.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two fields are easy to misread. <code>gitCredential</code> and <code>env</code> name
      environment variables — they do not hold values. The values live in an encrypted secret
      store, populated separately with <code>gitwho secret set</code>. That split is deliberate:
      it is what lets <code>accounts.toml</code> itself be committed to a dotfiles repo, since
      there is nothing secret in it to leak.
    </p>
    <p>
      For complete worked examples across providers, see <a href="/docs/recipes/">recipes</a>.
      What follows is the annotated template gitwho ships, with every field explained inline.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={source} lang="toml" label="the annotated accounts.toml template" />
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
